// `std::ofstream` is an ALIAS of `basic_ofstream<char>`, and a type request spelled as an alias
// carries no explicit instantiation, so the specialization used to arrive declaration-only and the
// name was reported as "cannot find the type 'std.ofstream'". The name resolves now, and the local
// CONSTRUCTS (the virtual-base constructor goes through a placement-new thunk). What this test
// pins is that the alias reaches the specialization's own MEMBER SURFACE: only a fully resolved
// basic_ofstream<char> knows its copy constructor is deleted. A declaration-only arrival fails
// earlier, with the type-not-found wording, which is the discriminator for the original defect.
import cpp "fstream";

extern int main()
{
    expect_error("its copy constructor is deleted") {
        std.ofstream m83stream = default;
        std.ofstream m83copy = m83stream;
    }
    return 0;
}
